{#- #153, 24 Aug 2026. /#pricing was a nav item pointing at an anchor on the
    homepage. This is the page it should have been.

    THE ONE RULE THIS PAGE MUST NOT BREAK: the price CTA is one press to the
    place money is taken. Until #1097 that was a field here and then Stripe;
    since #1097 (the operator's approved journey) it is the studio's sign-up,
    where the buyer signs in, answers website, what to find and plan, and then
    meets Stripe. The markup is the homepage tile's, ids and all, and the
    homepage tile keeps id="pricing" because deep links still land there. -#}

{#- The one page on this site with a price on it, and until #137 the only
    machine-readable thing about it was the word "Pricing" in the <title>.
    `Product` + `Offer` is the type Google shows a price for, and the numbers
    here are not transcribed from the copy above: `99900` USD is what
    netlify/functions/create-checkout-session.js actually sends to Stripe
    (`unit_amount` and `currency`), which is the only figure that cannot drift
    away from what a buyer is charged. test/seo.test.js pins the two together,
    so changing the price in one place and not the other fails the suite.

    `priceValidUntil` is deliberately absent: it is optional for a subscription
    that has no end date, and an invented one expires.

    #674, 15 Sep 2026. `brand` and `seller` used to be bare `{ "@id": … }`
    pointers at the Organization node base.njk puts on every page. That reads
    correctly as JSON-LD and Search Console still rejected it — "Invalid object
    type for field brand" — because the pointer crosses into a second <script>
    block and Google's parser did not resolve it there. `brand` is now an
    inline `Brand`, which is the shape Google's own Product documentation
    shows. `seller` keeps its `@id` AND states its type inline, so a parser
    that merges the graph still unifies the two and one that does not still
    sees a typed Organization.

    `hasMerchantReturnPolicy` says, machine-readably, the promise this page
    already makes in words twice: money back if it doesn't earn its keep, by
    email, with no window to miss. `MerchantReturnUnlimitedWindow` is that
    promise in Google's vocabulary and it is the honest one — the FAQ below
    says "write to us and we refund you" with no clock on it, which is a
    genuinely unlimited window rather than a generous finite one.
    `applicableCountry` is absent on purpose — Stripe takes a card from any
    country, Google has no wildcard for that, and listing a handful would
    narrow a promise we do not narrow. That is Google's Option B: a
    `merchantReturnLink` alone satisfies the requirement, and it points here,
    where the guarantee is written out, localized per language. #748 re-argued
    the same field on 17 Sep and put it to the operator; see below.

    FIVE FIELDS SEARCH CONSOLE ASKS FOR ARE MISSING ON PURPOSE. Do not
    "fix" them. Every one is a non-critical suggestion — it suppresses
    nothing, and the console will keep listing it — and every one of them
    would mean publishing something this page cannot honestly say. #748
    re-argued all five on 17 Sep 2026, when the dashboard asked again, and
    the answers below are what a later thread should read before typing a
    field in to make a warning go away.

      - `shippingDetails`: nothing is shipped. The field exists so Google can
        print a delivery cost and a delivery date; a workspace has neither.
        And schema.org's own way of SAYING nothing ships does not fit either:
        `doesNotShip` is defined per `shippingDestination`, "shipping to a
        particular shippingDestination is not available", so publishing it
        against everywhere is a negative shipping claim on a product that has
        no shipping. It reads as unavailable rather than as digital, which is
        worse than the warning.
      - `returnMethod`: schema.org holds the true value and Google does not
        read it. `KeepProduct` — "the consumer can keep the product, even when
        receiving a refund" — is exactly what a refunded month here is: the
        money goes back and nothing else moves. But Google's return-policy
        documentation supports three values, `ReturnByMail`, `ReturnInStore`
        and `ReturnAtKiosk`, and a refunded login is none of them. So
        publishing `KeepProduct` would not clear the suggestion, and it would
        put an enum value Google does not list into a field it does read —
        the same class of defect as #674's unresolved `@id`, traded for
        nothing. The day Google's supported list grows that fourth member this
        becomes one line, and it is the honest one.
      - `applicableCountry`: THE OPERATOR'S CALL, not a thread's, and filed as
        #749. Google takes up to 50 ISO country codes here and has no wildcard
        for "everywhere", so any list published tells Google the refund applies
        in those countries and, by omission, nowhere else. `/pricing/` promises
        money back with no territory on it, `/terms/` §02 carries the same
        promise unnarrowed (#680, deliberately), and Stripe takes a card from
        anywhere — so writing a list HERE would narrow a headline commercial
        term in the one place no customer would ever read it. If the promise is
        worldwide, this warning is permanent and correct. If it is not, the
        copy on `/pricing/` and the clause in `/terms/` change first and this
        markup follows them, never the other way round.
      - `aggregateRating` and `review`: Prospektor has no customers yet, so it
        has no ratings and no reviews. Writing either would be fabricated
        review markup — against Google's structured data policy, grounds for a
        manual action against the whole domain, and a false claim to a person
        reading a search result. When real customers give real reviews, these
        two become the right thing to add, and not one day before.

    #542: two Offers, because there are now two ways to buy the same one
    workspace. `offers` takes a list for exactly this, and the test above reads
    BOTH against the PLANS table in the function — so a plan whose figure moves
    in one place and not the other fails the suite rather than reaching a
    search result. -#}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Product",
  "name": "Prospektor",
  "description": {{ ("A Prospektor workspace: leads, research, decision-makers with sources, warm intros and every pitch written in your voice." | t) | dump | safe }},
  "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": "{{ site.name }}" },
  "url": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}",
  "image": "{{ site.url }}{{ '/assets/img/og.png' | asset }}",
  "offers": [
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      "name": {{ ("Monthly" | t) | dump | safe }},
      "url": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}",
      "price": "999.00",
      "priceCurrency": "USD",
      "availability": "https://schema.org/InStock",
      "seller": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization", "@type": "Organization", "name": "{{ site.name }}" },
      "hasMerchantReturnPolicy": {
        "@type": "MerchantReturnPolicy",
        "returnPolicyCategory": "https://schema.org/MerchantReturnUnlimitedWindow",
        "returnFees": "https://schema.org/FreeReturn",
        "refundType": "https://schema.org/FullRefund",
        "merchantReturnLink": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}"
      },
      "priceSpecification": {
        "@type": "UnitPriceSpecification",
        "price": "999.00",
        "priceCurrency": "USD",
        "billingIncrement": 1,
        "unitText": "workspace",
        "billingDuration": 1,
        "billingPeriod": "P1M"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      "name": {{ ("Yearly" | t) | dump | safe }},
      "url": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}",
      "price": "9990.00",
      "priceCurrency": "USD",
      "availability": "https://schema.org/InStock",
      "seller": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization", "@type": "Organization", "name": "{{ site.name }}" },
      "hasMerchantReturnPolicy": {
        "@type": "MerchantReturnPolicy",
        "returnPolicyCategory": "https://schema.org/MerchantReturnUnlimitedWindow",
        "returnFees": "https://schema.org/FreeReturn",
        "refundType": "https://schema.org/FullRefund",
        "merchantReturnLink": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}"
      },
      "priceSpecification": {
        "@type": "UnitPriceSpecification",
        "price": "9990.00",
        "priceCurrency": "USD",
        "billingIncrement": 1,
        "unitText": "workspace",
        "billingDuration": 1,
        "billingPeriod": "P1Y"
      }
    }
  ]
}
</script>

<header class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <h1 class="page-h1">{% t %}One price for your whole team{% endt %}</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">{% t %}Who to pitch is free. What to send is one workspace, with no seats to count and no credit meter.{% endt %}</p>
  </div>
</header>

{#- #1092: the two-card shape the homepage uses, the thesis priced. The free
    card is the scan and the free run (#419); the paid card is the one place
    on this page with a coral button. -#}
<section class="sec sec-first pricing-page" id="plans">
  <div class="col">
    <div class="grid2 price">
      <div class="hcard">
        <p class="hcard-q">{% t %}Who to pitch{% endt %}</p>
        <b class="big">{% t %}Free{% endt %}</b>
        <p class="per">{% t %}No card, no sign-up to start{% endt %}</p>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>{% t %}Your site read, and what you should hunt for{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Three companies that fit, scored{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Who buys from one of your competitors{% endt %}</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="hcard-foot"><a href="{{ '/#scan' | localize }}">{% t %}Scan your site{% endt %} ›</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="hcard pricing-card">
        {#- #542, the yearly plan. A SEGMENTED CONTROL, not a second buy button:
            a pricing page gets one primary action (the style skill, rule 8).
            Both figures and both sentences are written here, by the build, in
            the reader's language; plan.js only decides which of them is
            hidden. With JavaScript off the page is the monthly page, which is
            also what a crawler reads. -#}
        <p class="hcard-q">{% t %}What to send{% endt %}</p>
        <div class="plan-switch" id="planSwitch" role="group" aria-label="{% t %}Billing period{% endt %}">
          <button type="button" class="plan-opt" data-plan="month" aria-pressed="true">{% t %}Monthly{% endt %}</button>
          <button type="button" class="plan-opt" data-plan="year" aria-pressed="false">{% t %}Yearly (2 months free){% endt %}</button>
        </div>
        <div class="price-row" data-plan-show="month">
          <span class="price-num">$999</span>
          <span class="price-per">{% t %}/ month · per workspace{% endt %}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="price-row" data-plan-show="year" hidden>
          <span class="price-num">$9,990</span>
          <span class="price-per">{% t %}/ year · per workspace{% endt %}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="plan-note" data-plan-show="month">{% t %}Or <strong>$9,990 a year</strong>, two months free.{% endt %}</p>
        <p class="plan-note" data-plan-show="year" hidden>{% t %}<strong>Two months free</strong> on $11,988, and the price is fixed for the year.{% endt %}</p>

        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>{% t %}Leads for you, Research and Counterprospekt{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Every pitch your team needs: deck, one-pager, cold email, proposal{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Decision-makers, with a source behind every name{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Warm intros from your network, with both emails drafted{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Replies to inbound, and a sheet for every call{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Every colleague on your work-email domain, at no extra cost{% endt %}</li>
        </ul>

        {#- #1097. One link to the studio's sign-up. The plan the switch shows
            rides on it as `plan=yearly`, written by signup.js. -#}
        <div class="buy" id="buy">
          <a href="{{ site.studio }}/signup{% if language.code != 'en' %}?lang={{ language.code }}{% endif %}" class="btn-cta" id="buyLink" data-signup>{% t %}Start my studio →{% endt %}</a>
          <p class="buy-note">
            {#- #423, and it changes with the FAQ answer below. -#}
            {% t %}Money back if it doesn't earn its keep, with no fourteen days of hoping you remember to cancel.{% endt %}
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{#- defer (#137). Every one of these is an IIFE that looks its elements up by
    id and returns if they are absent. signup.js loads BEFORE plan.js on
    purpose (#542, #1097): deferred scripts run in document order, so it is
    listening for the switch's `plan` event by the time plan.js announces the
    choice a `?plan=` in the URL already made. -#}
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/signup.js' | asset }}" defer></script>
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/plan.js' | asset }}" defer></script>

<section class="pricing-faq">
  <div class="pricing-faq-inner">
    <h2>{% t %}The money questions{% endt %}</h2>
    <dl class="pricing-faq-list">
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}What counts as a workspace?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}One company you are selling <em>for</em>: its brief, its voice, its
          proof points, its pitches, its network. Selling for yourself means one
          workspace. An agency selling on behalf of five clients keeps five, so
          nobody's research, voice or network leaks into anybody else's.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}How many seats do I get?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}All of them. Pay with your work email and every colleague on that
          domain signs straight in. There is nothing to count and nothing to
          upgrade. Sign-in is Google or an emailed single-use link; there are no
          passwords.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}Is there a run limit or a credit meter?{% endt %}</dt>
        {#- #1092. This said "Runs are unlimited" until the studio's own help
            documented daily ceilings (100 glances a day per workspace, for
            one). The help is the place that lists them; this names one. -#}
        <dd>{% t %}There is no credit meter, and runs are included in the price. A few
          presses have a daily ceiling per workspace, such as 100 glances a day,
          so nobody's runaway script spends your team's day. The
          <a href="/help/">help</a> lists each one.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        {#- #423. The old answer said "no trial theater" and named the scan as
            the only free thing. Both had gone stale in different directions:
            #419 put a second free thing on the page (the run at /r), and #422
            built a free tier whose door opens on one operator env var. This
            answer is written to survive that flip — it denies the fourteen-day
            clock, which stays true, rather than denying that anything is free,
            which does not. -#}
        <dt>{% t %}Is there a free trial?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}No clock, no. The scan on the homepage reads your own site and shows
          who it would hunt for you, and the
          <a href="https://studio.prospektor.ai/r">free run</a> names companies
          worth approaching. Neither asks for an account or a card. The
          subscription you pay for up front and take the money back if it
          doesn't earn its keep, with no fourteen days of hoping you remember to
          cancel.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}What does "money back if it doesn't earn its keep" mean?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}Exactly what it says. Write to
          <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a> and we refund
          you. We would rather hear why than keep a month you regret.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        {#- #542. The one thing the yearly plan does NOT do yet: move a
            workspace that is already paying. Saying so on the page it is sold
            from is cheaper than a support thread that starts with a broken
            expectation, and it is the only honest answer until billing is
            self-serve. -#}
        <dt>{% t %}Can I switch between monthly and yearly?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}Yes. Email <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a> and
          we move you at your next renewal. There is no switch inside the studio
          yet, so today it is a reply from a human rather than a button.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}What happens if I stop?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}Nothing is held hostage. Export what you have, share the links you
          have shared, print what you need, and the workspace and everything in
          it is deleted when you ask. What we sell is next month's work, not
          custody of last month's.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="pricing-faq-item">
        <dt>{% t %}What happens to my data?{% endt %}</dt>
        <dd>{% t %}Workspaces are strictly separated: your research, pitches and
          network are visible to your team only. Email addresses in a LinkedIn
          export are deleted at import, and your network never travels on a
          shared pitch. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> says the rest,
          plainly.{% endt %}</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>
